# ── Network ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# testnet | mainnet
WATERX_NETWORK=testnet

# ── Wallet ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bech32 Ed25519 secret key (suiprivkey1...). `pnpm run generate-wallet` creates one.
SUI_PRIVATE_KEY=

# The WaterX account this agent trades. `pnpm run create-account` creates it;
# `pnpm run accounts` prints the id once the indexer has picked it up.
WATERX_ACCOUNT_ID=

# ── Execution policy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Gates every signature this process can produce.
#   read-only       every write throws before a signature exists
#   interactive     a write needs an explicit per-call confirmation (`--yes`)
#   delegated-auto  unattended signing inside a scope file — delegate wallets only
# Unset defaults to `interactive` on testnet and `read-only` on mainnet.
# `--policy <mode>` narrows for one invocation; it can never widen.
WATERX_EXECUTION_POLICY=interactive

# Required by delegated-auto. A JSON document of mandatory ceilings — see
# policy.example.json. Nothing enforces a per-order limit server-side on the perp
# line, so this file is the only amount ceiling a delegate has.
# WATERX_POLICY_SCOPE_FILE=./policy.json

# ── Signer (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset, the key is read from SUI_PRIVATE_KEY into this process — fine at a
# terminal, wrong for anything that signs while nobody is watching.
#
# Set, the key lives in a child process and this one never sees it. The wire is
# SIGNER_PROTOCOL v1, so any conforming provider serves — including the predict
# line's keystore agent, which needs no changes:
#   WATERX_SIGNER_COMMAND=["waterx-predict-keystore","sign"]
#   WATERX_AGENT_WALLET=0x…       # the address the child holds; stated, not derived
#   WATERX_SIGNER_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# See examples/keypair-signer.mjs for the smallest provider that satisfies it.

# ── Delegate mode (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set ONLY when SUI_PRIVATE_KEY is a delegate key. The owner stays the
# authorisation subject; this wallet signs. Funds-out paths remain owner-only on
# chain, so a delegate can trade the account but cannot withdraw from it.
# WATERX_OWNER_ADDRESS=

# ── Endpoints (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults are derived from WATERX_NETWORK; override only to point at staging or
# a local backend.
# WATERX_API_URL=https://api-testnet.waterx.app
# SUI_GRPC_URL=https://fullnode.testnet.sui.io:443
# WATERX_CONFIG_URL=https://staging.waterx-config.pages.dev/testnet.json

# Package ids to accept on top of the ones the deployment document lists.
#
# Every Move call in a transaction must belong to a package this agent can name;
# an unknown one is refused before signing, because a single call from a package
# nobody vouches for can act on the shared objects with the signer's authority.
#
# The deployment document does not currently list every package the backend
# calls, so this exists as a stopgap: naming the exception keeps it written down
# and removable, rather than accepting every unknown package silently. Run
# `pnpm run doctor` — it prints the exact line for the network you are on.
#
# Three forms, granting different amounts:
#
#   0xPKG                       the package may appear as a TYPE argument.
#                               Covers no call: a type never executes.
#   0xPKG=@waterx/perp::trading calls to that module are accepted AND checked
#                               against the SDK's declared function and arity.
#   0xPKG=*                     every call from that package is accepted with
#                               nothing holding it to a shape.
#
# `=*` is deliberately the ugliest, because it gives up the most. It exists for
# third-party packages: mainnet's order path calls Pyth Lazer, whose functions
# no @waterx/sdk release declares, so there is no declaration to check against.
# It is still only that ONE package — wider about what a package may do, never
# about which package it is.
#
# On mainnet today, every order needs the Pyth Lazer package named this way, and
# the USDC / reward coin types need their bare ids. `doctor` prints all three.
#
# WATERX_EXTRA_PACKAGES=0x...=waterx_rule::waterx_rule,0x...

# How stale a deployment manifest may be when the document cannot be re-read.
#
# Zero unless set. The manifest is re-read every five minutes; if that fails,
# signing refuses rather than trade on a copy it cannot confirm — an upgrade
# DURING an outage is exactly when the held copy is wrong and exactly when it
# cannot be corrected. Set this in minutes to keep trading through an outage,
# knowing that. `pnpm run doctor` reports the allowance while it is in force.
# An unreadable value is refused rather than treated as "no limit": both `abc`
# and `Infinity` used to switch the guard off entirely. Capped at 24 hours.
# WATERX_MANIFEST_GRACE_MINUTES=30

# Accept an argument layout this deployment has never been seen to emit.
#
# Empty unless set, and there is no boolean form anywhere — not here and not in
# the exported config. A list of exact entrypoints is the only lever.
#
# Takes a comma-separated list of `module::function` entrypoints and nothing
# else — there is no blanket form, because accepting every unconfirmed layout at
# once is wider than any reason for wanting one, and would put back the
# entrypoints whose objects have never been observed alongside the merely
# uncaptured. `pnpm run doctor` prints the exact line to paste.
#
# EVERY action is covered, exits included. An exit whose layout is unconfirmed
# is urgent — it means the corpus does not cover the way out — but "reduces
# risk" says nothing about whether an argument is read from the right slot, so
# it is not a reason to skip the check. Capture it, or name it here.
#
# Names are case-sensitive and must exist: a misspelling is refused rather than
# silently allowing nothing.
# WATERX_ALLOW_UNCONFIRMED_ABI=lp_pool::request_redeem

# ── The approval path (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# `preview` writes a plan here, `approve` records a person's decision on it, and
# `execute` submits it unchanged. An append-only JSONL file, gitignored, and the
# reason the three can be separate processes at all.
# WATERX_APPROVALS_FILE=.waterx/approvals.jsonl

# How long a previewed plan stays approvable. It expires because the prices it
# was derived from go stale, and approving an old quote approves an order nobody
# would agree to now. Default 600 seconds.
# WATERX_APPROVAL_TTL_SECONDS=600

# Every transaction digest, written BEFORE the bytes leave the process. This is
# what makes a crash or a timeout recoverable: `pnpm run reconcile` asks the
# chain about exactly these. Losing it means guessing, and guessing wrong means
# trading twice.
# WATERX_SUBMISSIONS_FILE=.waterx/submissions.jsonl
